const multiBuildingNarrative = {
  eyebrow: 'ONE PARCEL, MANY KITCHENS',
  headline: 'Hope Ranch Properties Are Appliance Fleets, Not Households',
  paragraphs: [
    "Count the refrigeration on a typical Hope Ranch parcel: the main kitchen's built-in, the butler's pantry column or drawers, the pool house undercounter, the guest house's full-size unit, a beverage center at the outdoor kitchen, maybe a garage freezer holding the overflow. That's six compressors on one property — a small fleet, each unit with its own age, duty cycle, and environment.",
    "Fleets deserve fleet thinking. The pool house undercounter that only runs hard in summer fails differently from the garage freezer that runs year-round in an unconditioned space, and differently again from the pampered main-kitchen built-in. When we service one unit, we log what we saw on the others worth glancing at — a gasket going soft here, a condenser loading up there — so the property has an actual picture of its inventory instead of a series of surprises.",
    "The outbuildings are where problems hide. Main kitchens get noticed; the guest house refrigerator gets discovered — usually by a guest. A twice-a-year walkthrough of the secondary units costs one service visit and catches the quiet failures while they're cheap. Several Hope Ranch properties run exactly that arrangement with us, and their 'appliance emergencies' have mostly stopped being emergencies."
  ]
};

